You shouldn't trust an AoLS to be complete protection, but if you don't
need your amulet slot for anything else, you ought to wear one.  
SDSM and either an artifact that grants magic resistance (if you're
paranoid about sea creatures) or a CoMR (if you're not, or if you're
just going to genocide them) gives you reflection with your amulet 
slot unused, as does GDSM with a [oReflection.

There really aren't that many important types of amulets in the
later game.  If you go through the amulet list:

Amulets of change, restful sleep, strangulation, imitation AoY - 
  obviously will not wear those.
Amulet of unchanging - If you have MR, only useful if you have some 
  reason to want to stay polymorphed.  Not a usual case.
Amulet versus poison - Characters will almost always have poison
  resistance by the time they have many choices of amulets to wear.
Amulet of Yendor - Can be worn on the planes in a pinch, but otherwise
  not.

So that leaves ESP, life saving, reflection, and magical breathing as
amulets that could potentially be worn generally during the later parts
of the game.  Reflection is of course the best choice if you don't have any
other means of reflection.  Magical breathing is really only useful in
occasional circumstances, and it won't hurt anything to get switch to
it in those cases.

ESP does give you an advantage, as it helps you spot monsters that you
wouldn't normally be able to see.  I tend to think that it's not that useful
once you've put together a full set of equipment (by that point, most of
the monsters worth worrying about teleport right to you anyway), but earlier
on it may keep you out of trouble.

Lifesaving essentially doubles your hitpoints, and also will save your life
if you do something like accidentally touch a cockatrice corpse, or get
drowned by a sea monster.  Sure, maybe you'll go and get yourself killed
again immediately, but at least you've got a chance to fix the problem if
you were lifesaved.

I typically will wear lifesaving if I'm not using the slot for reflection.
It's not worth choosing the equipment you're using specifically to allow you
to wear it, but if I can, why not?


